+++

I personally make use of [NixOS][nixos] with [home-manager][hm] to manage my different systems. I
think it's really nice, but the goal of this post isn't to sell you NixOS (or Nix in general).

I believe I found a better, nicer way to achieve what we want

[nixos]: https://nixos.org
[hm]: https://github.com/nix-community/home-manager

## The current "status quo"

Most users will set this up by following the [example setup][hm-example-nixos]. In short, you get
the home-manager NixOS module (either with channels or flakes), and import it in your system config:

[hm-example-nixos]: https://nix-community.github.io/home-manager/nix-flakes/nixos.html

```nix
{ ... }: let
  home-manager = import <home-manager>;
  # or use flakes with inputs.home-manager
in {

  imports = [
    home-manager.nixosModules.default
  ];

  users.users."nferhat" = {
    isNormalUser = true;
    extraGroups = ["wheel"];
  };

  home-manager = {
    useGlobalPkgs = true;
    useUserPackages = true;
    extraSpecialArgs = {inherit self inputs;};
    users."nferhat" = /path/to/home.nix;
  };
}
```

Then, in `home.nix` you would use the home-manager provided modules.

```nix
{ ... }: {
  imports = [fht-compositor.homeModules.default];
  programs.neovim.enable = true;
  programs.fht-compositor.settings = { ... };
}
```

However, this introduces a hard split between your system config and user config that should not exist.
They both should be coupled (which in practice, _it is_), but semantically, it is not: they exist in
different files/modules. \
When changing/adding something to your home configuration, you don't immediatly think "hmm maybe this
also needs some system service"

Moreover, a lot of options/modules that are _included_ in home-manager the issue of needing something,
in the system configuration. For example, setting the GTK3/4 theme require [`dconf`][dconf] to be installed and available in the
systemd services' `$PATH`

[dconf]: https://wiki.gnome.org/Projects/dconf

## What do we do then?

> **NOTE**: What I am about to say isn't particularly new or "original", but I still believe I
added some nice value to make this system work better.

What I resorted to is to couple my home-manager and system configuration together, in "modules": not
the ones that declare options, but instead as configuration pieces imported by my hosts.

You could already do this using `home-managers.users."name"`{=nix}, but adding an alias makes it much
easier/faster to type. This is pretty straightforward todo: in your system config, add this line:

```nix
{ lib, ... }: {
  imports = [(lib.mkAliasOptionModule ["nferhat"] ["home-manager" "users" "nferhat"])]
}
```

This also adds a way to ---at a glance--- know if I'm configuring for my system or for my user. (based
on the presence/absence of the `<name>` prefix), which helps very much since both HM and NixOS modules
share the same conventions: `programs.*`, `services.*`, ...

However, this still isn't the most ergonomic, there's a lot of redundancy, especially when using using
stuff inside `home.*`: `nferhat.home.packages`, `nferhat.home.shellAliases`, etc.:

The solution is blissfully simple: *more* aliases! This time scoped for home-manager:

```nix
{
  nferhat.imports = [
    # Aliases to make things even more streamlined.
    (lib.mkAliasOptionModule ["packages"] ["home" "packages"])
    (lib.mkAliasOptionModule ["shellAliases"] ["home" "shellAliases"])
    (lib.mkAliasOptionModule ["configFile"] ["xdg" "configFile"])
  ];
}
```

These are the ones I set up for now, and now configuring stuff is really, really nice, take for
example my neovim config:

```nix
{
  pkgs,
  lib,
  ...
}: {
  # This is NixOS' neovim module.
  # This does nothing special. Sets $EDITOR, and makes sure plugins installed in the $NIX_PATH can
  # be loaded by neovim. User configuration is linked from the flake.
  programs.neovim = {
    enable = true;
    viAlias = true;
    vimAlias = true;
  };

  # needed to build TS grammars.
  environment.systemPackages = [pkgs.gcc pkgs.tree-sitter];
  nferhat.configFile."nvim".source = lib.fht.linkTo "/my/nvim/config";

  # --- snip: other stuff for custom theme, doesnt matter. much ---
}
```
This is self-contained, simple, and easy to understand!

{summary="Note on `lib.fht`"}
::: details
`lib.fht.linkTo` is essentially a clone of [`mkOutOfStoreSymlink`]{.function}, which you should
be familiar enough if you used home-manager before.

This is one downside of this approach is that you lose access to home-manager lib, which includes
DAG stuff if you use that. I don't, so this doesn't affect + `mkOutOfStoreSymlink` is pretty simple
to copy, it only depends on another small function.
:::

## Conclusion

Overall, I like this! I've restructured all my [dotfiles][dots] to use this new system. It works well
enough and I've yet to notice any downsides:

